## The timeline, as an app would draw it

<!-- l4: decide:the death event in -->

**69.** In relation to an Administration “a”, the death event in means Timeline event where date is dod, event is the death of the deceased and basis is PAA s 37: on an intestacy, and on a testacy where no executor has power to obtain probate, the real and personal estate vests in the Public Trustee from this date until a grant of administration is made..

For the purposes of this provision—

“dod” means a's case's deceased's date of death;

<!-- l4: decide:the section 55(1)(a) event in -->

**70.** In relation to an Administration “a”, the section 55(1)(a) event in means Timeline event where date is due, event is 6 months from the death: if no application for probate or letters of administration has been made, letters of administration may be granted to the Public Trustee or to such other person as the court thinks fit and basis is PAA s 55(1)(a). By PAA s 55(2) the Public Trustee is not prevented from applying before this date..

For the purposes of this provision—

“due” means the date 6 months after the death in with a;

<!-- l4: decide:the grant event on -->

**71.** In relation to a DATE “d”, the grant event on means Timeline event where date is d, event is the grant is made and basis is PAA s 28(1): upon the grant the grantee takes an oath faithfully to administer the estate and to account for it, unless the grantee is the Public Trustee or a trust company (s 28(2)). PAA s 37(4): the estate ceases to be vested in the Public Trustee..

<!-- l4: decide:the section 55(1)(c) event on -->

**72.** In relation to a DATE “d”, the section 55(1)(c) event on means Timeline event where date is add months with d and 1, event is one month from the grant: security lawfully required must have been given, or letters of administration may be granted to the Public Trustee and basis is PAA s 55(1)(c), read with PAA s 29(7) and s 29(8) on when security is required at all..

<!-- l4: decide:the Wills Act section 28(2) event on -->

**73.** In relation to a DATE “due”, the Wills Act section 28(2) event on means Timeline event where date is due, event is 6 months from the date the grant is first made: last date for an application to rectify the will without the permission of a court and basis is WA 1838 s 28(2). The court may permit a later application; WA s 28(3) protects personal representatives who distribute after this date..

<!-- l4: decide:the section 55(1)(f) event on -->

**74.** In relation to a DATE “d”, the section 55(1)(f) event on means Timeline event where date is add months with d and 6, event is 6 months from the death of the last surviving executor or administrator: if no application for the representation of the estate has been made, letters of administration may be granted to the Public Trustee and basis is PAA s 55(1)(f), read with PAA s 25(2) under which the court is guided by the same rules as apply to original grants..

<!-- l4: decide:the timeline in -->

**75.** In relation to an Administration “a”, the timeline in means append with fromDeath and append with fromGrant and append with fromRectification and fromSecondDeath.

For the purposes of this provision—

“death” means the death event in with a;

“sixMonths” means the section 55(1)(a) event in with a;

“fromDeath” means list of death and sixMonths;

“fromGrant” means consider the case distinctions of a's date of the grant :  when NOTHING has  then EMPTY. when JUST has d then list of the grant event on with d and the section 55(1)(c) event on with d;

“fromRectification” means consider the case distinctions of the last date for an application to rectify the will in with a :  when NOTHING has  then EMPTY. when JUST has due then list of the Wills Act section 28(2) event on with due;

“fromSecondDeath” means consider the case distinctions of a's date of death of the last surviving executor or administrator :  when NOTHING has  then EMPTY. when JUST has d then list of the section 55(1)(f) event on with d;

## Part 11 — General: what comes off the top, and in what order

<!-- l4: decide:First Schedule paragraph 1 -->

**76.** First Schedule paragraph 1 means Rule of application where paragraph is 1 and text is The funeral, testamentary and administration expenses shall have priority..

<!-- l4: decide:First Schedule paragraph 2 -->

**77.** First Schedule paragraph 2 means Rule of application where paragraph is 2 and text is Subject to paragraph 1, the same rules shall prevail and be observed as to the respective rights of secured and unsecured creditors and as to debts and liabilities provable and as to the valuation of annuities and future and contingent liabilities respectively, and as to the priorities of debts and liabilities, as may be in force for the time being under the law of bankruptcy with respect to the assets of persons adjudged bankrupt..

<!-- l4: decide:the First Schedule -->

**78.** The First Schedule means list of First Schedule paragraph 1 and First Schedule paragraph 2.

<!-- l4: decide:Second Schedule item 1 -->

**79.** Second Schedule item 1 means Rule of application where paragraph is 1 and text is Property of the deceased undisposed of by will, subject to the retention thereout of a fund sufficient to meet any pecuniary legacies..

<!-- l4: decide:Second Schedule item 2 -->

**80.** Second Schedule item 2 means Rule of application where paragraph is 2 and text is Property of the deceased not specifically devised or bequeathed but included (either by a specific or general description) in a residuary gift, subject to the retention out of such property of a fund sufficient to meet any pecuniary legacies, so far as not provided for as aforesaid..

<!-- l4: decide:Second Schedule item 3 -->

**81.** Second Schedule item 3 means Rule of application where paragraph is 3 and text is Property of the deceased specifically appropriated or devised or bequeathed (either by a specific or general description) for the payment of debts..

<!-- l4: decide:Second Schedule item 4 -->

**82.** Second Schedule item 4 means Rule of application where paragraph is 4 and text is Property of the deceased charged with, or devised or bequeathed (either by a specific or general description) subject to a charge for the payment of debts..

<!-- l4: decide:Second Schedule item 5 -->

**83.** Second Schedule item 5 means Rule of application where paragraph is 5 and text is The fund, if any, retained to meet pecuniary legacies..

<!-- l4: decide:Second Schedule item 6 -->

**84.** Second Schedule item 6 means Rule of application where paragraph is 6 and text is Property specifically devised or bequeathed, rateably according to value..

<!-- l4: decide:Second Schedule item 7 -->

**85.** Second Schedule item 7 means Rule of application where paragraph is 7 and text is Property appointed by will under a general power, rateably according to value..

<!-- l4: decide:Second Schedule item 8 -->

**86.** Second Schedule item 8 means Rule of application where paragraph is 8 and text is The following provisions shall also apply: (a) the order of application may be varied by the will of the deceased; and (b) this Schedule does not affect the liability of land to answer the death duty imposed thereon in exoneration of other assets..

<!-- l4: decide:the Second Schedule -->

**87.** The Second Schedule means list of Second Schedule item 1, Second Schedule item 2, Second Schedule item 3, Second Schedule item 4, Second Schedule item 5, Second Schedule item 6, Second Schedule item 7 and Second Schedule item 8.

<!-- l4: decide:the application of assets in -->

**88.** In relation to an Administration “a”, the application of assets in means if the estate is solvent with c, then Application of assets where solvent is TRUE, schedule is Second Schedule, rules is the Second Schedule and basis is PAA s 57(4): where the estate of a deceased person is solvent his estate shall, subject to the Rules of Court and section 58 as to charges on property of the deceased, and to the provisions, if any, contained in his will, be applicable towards the discharge of the funeral, testamentary and administration expenses, debts and liabilities payable thereout in the order mentioned in the Second Schedule.; otherwise, Application of assets where solvent is FALSE, schedule is First Schedule, rules is the First Schedule and basis is PAA s 57(1): where the estate of a deceased person is insolvent his estate shall be administered in accordance with the rules set out in the First Schedule. Funeral, testamentary and administration expenses have priority; below them the bankruptcy priorities govern..

For the purposes of this provision—

“c” means a's case;

<!-- l4: decide:the schedule that applies in -->

**89.** In relation to an Administration “a”, the schedule that applies in means r's schedule.

For the purposes of this provision—

“r” means the application of assets in with a;

<!-- l4: decide:the order of application of assets in -->

**90.** In relation to an Administration “a”, the order of application of assets in means r's rules.

For the purposes of this provision—

“r” means the application of assets in with a;

<!-- l4: decide:the statutory order governs in -->

**91.** In relation to an Administration “a”, the statutory order governs in if a's the order of application is not varied by the will of the deceased.

<!-- l4: decide:the interest charged is primarily liable in -->

**92.** In relation to a Charged interest “i”, the interest charged is primarily liable in if—

    (a) i's charged with the payment of money at the time of his death; and

    (b) a contrary intention is not signified in with i.

<!-- l4: decide:a contrary intention is signified in -->

**93.** In relation to a Charged interest “i”, a contrary intention is signified in if—

    (a) i's a contrary or other intention signified by will, deed or other document; and

    (b) it is not the case that i's that intention signified only by a general direction for the payment of debts or by a charge of debts upon the residuary estate.

<!-- l4: decide:the proportionate part of the charge borne by -->

**94.** In relation to a Charged interest “i”, a NUMBER “part” and a NUMBER “charge”, the proportionate part of the charge borne by means if whole is greater than 0, then the product of charge and the result of dividing part by whole; otherwise, 0.

For the purposes of this provision—

“whole” means i's value;

<!-- l4: decide:the section 66 maximum rate -->

**95.** The section 66 maximum rate means the result of dividing 5 by 100.

<!-- l4: decide:the maximum commission under section 66 in -->

**96.** In relation to an Administration “a”, the maximum commission under section 66 in means the product of the section 66 maximum rate and collected.

For the purposes of this provision—

“collected” means a's the value of the assets collected by them;

<!-- l4: decide:the heads of expense the court shall allow under section 67 -->

**97.** The heads of expense the court shall allow under section 67 means list of the reasonable testamentary and other expenses incurred by them, proper funeral expenses and all reasonable expenses of subsequent religious ceremonies suitable to the station in life of the deceased.

<!-- l4: decide:the matter is uncontested in -->

**98.** In relation to an Administration “a”, the matter is uncontested in if—

    (a) a's a probate action is not pending; and

    (b) it is not the case that a caveat bars the grant in with a.

<!-- l4: decide:the registrar may exercise the court's powers in -->

**99.** In relation to an Administration “a”, the registrar may exercise the court's powers in means the matter is uncontested in with a.

## Fixtures — the facts the assertions are asserted about

<!-- l4: decide:an able and willing executor named -->

